wo different lines (since a line cannot cover both). So we need two lines to cover (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Those two lines could be the two sunny lines. Then we need to also cover (n,1). That would require a third line (sunny or non-sunny). But we have only two sunny lines left, and we already used them for the two points in column n-1. So we cannot cover (n,1) with a sunny line because we have no more sunny lines. Could we cover (n,1) with the non-sunny line? The non-sunny line is vertical x=n-2, which does not cover (n,1). So we need a third line, but we only have one non-sunny line left (which we used as vertical x=n-2). So we need to adjust: maybe we don't use vertical line x=n-2; maybe we use a non-sunny line that can cover (n,1) as well. For example, horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) covers (n,1) and also (n-1,1) and (n-2,1). But then we need to cover (n-1,2) and (n-2,2) and (n-2,3). Let's see if we can use vertical line x=n-2 to cover (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) (vertical line covers all three points in column n-2). But then (n-2,1) is also covered by vertical line, but also covered by horizontal line y=1. That's okay. So we could have vertical line x=n-2 (non-sunny) covering (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) covering (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1). Then we have left (n-1,2) uncovered. We have two sunny lines left (since we need exactly k=2). We can use one sunny line to cover (n-1,2) and maybe also some other point in a column we haven't used vertical lines for? But we have used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (if we want). Actually we haven't decided about columns 1,...,n-3. We could use vertical lines for those columns. So we have used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines). Then we have used vertical line x=n-2 (one more), horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny), and we have two sunny lines left to reach total n lines. Let's count: vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3: that's n-3 lines. Add vertical line x=n-2: that's 1 line, total n-2 lines. Add horizontal line y=1: that's 1 line, total n-1 lines. We need total n lines, so we have one more line to allocate, which must be sunny (since we need exactly k=2 sunny lines). So we have exactly 2 sunny lines left. We need to cover (n-1,2) and maybe some other points not yet covered. Let's see which points are uncovered after using vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3, vertical line x=n-2, horizontal line y=1.\n\n- For columns 1,...,n-3: vertical lines cover all points in those columns. So all points with x ≤ n-3 are covered.\n\n- Column n-2: vertical line x=n-2 covers all points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Horizontal line y=1 also covers (n-2,1) but that's okay.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1) is covered by horizontal line y=1. (n-1,2) is not covered yet.\n\n- Column n: point (n,1) is covered by horizontal line y=1.\n\nThus only uncovered point is (n-1,2). So we need to cover (n-1,2) with a line. We have two sunny lines left, but we need only one line to cover that point. So we can use one sunny line to cover (n-1,2). The other sunny line can be used arbitrarily (maybe covering some other point redundantly). But we must have exactly k=2 sunny lines, so we need to use both. The second sunny line could be any line that doesn't affect coverage (maybe a line through two already covered points). But we need to ensure it's not non-sunny (i.e., not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1). So we can choose a sunny line that passes through (n-1,2) and some other point not already covered? But (n-1,2) is already covered by the first sunny line; we could have the second sunny line be any line that passes through two points that are already covered, e.g., (1,1) and (2,2) (slope 1). That line is sunny (slope 1). It will cover those points, but they are already covered by vertical lines. That's fine. So we can have exactly 2 sunny lines: one covering (n-1,2) (maybe also some other point in a column where we have vertical lines, but that's okay), and another arbitrary sunny line covering 