,1): slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. L4: line through (1,3) and (2,1) covers those two points.\n\nThus we have 3 sunny lines L2, L3, L4 covering all remaining points. So we have a construction for n=4 with exactly 3 sunny lines. So k=3 is possible.\n\nNow for k≥4: The solution says impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column. Let's examine that claim. For n lines total, we have n columns (x=1,...,x=n). Actually columns from 1 to n. Each column i has points (i,1) up to (i, n+1-i). So column i has n+1-i points. For i ≤ n-2, column i has at least 3 points. For i = n-1, column has 2 points. For i = n, column has 1 point.\n\nIf we have k sunny lines, each sunny line can intersect each column at most once. So total number of points covered by sunny lines in column i is at most k (since each sunny line can cover at most one point in column i). But we also have non-sunny lines (vertical, horizontal, slope -1) that can cover many points in a column. However, the total number of lines is n, so we have n - k non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover many points in a column), horizontal (cover many points across columns), or slope -1 (cover many points across columns). The claim that k≥4 impossible because sunny lines cover at most one point per column is not sufficient; we need to consider the total number of columns that need to be covered by sunny lines. Let's examine the constraints more carefully.\n\nWe need to cover all points. For each column i, we need to cover all points in that column. Non-sunny lines can cover many points in a column if they are vertical lines (cover all points in that column). Horizontal lines can cover points across columns but only one per column (since horizontal line y = constant intersects each column at exactly one point). Slope -1 lines also intersect each column at exactly one point (since slope -1 line is not vertical). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover columns with 