,(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1).\n- L4: slope -1 line x+y=5 covering (2,3),(3,2),(4,1).\n- L5: horizontal y=1 covering (2,1),(3,1),(5,1) (since (4,1) covered by L4, (6,1) covered by L3, (7,1) covered by L2).\n- L6: horizontal y=2 covering (2,2),(3,2) (but (3,2) covered by L4), (4,2) not covered, (5,2) covered by L3, (6,2) covered by L2.\n\nNow let's see what's covered:\n\nColumn 2: points (2,1)-(2,6). Covered: (2,6) L2, (2,5) L3, (2,3) L4, (2,2) L6, (2,1) L5. Left (2,4) uncovered.\n\nColumn 3: points (3,1)-(3,5). Covered: (3,5) L2, (3,4) L3, (3,2) L4 and L6, (3,1) L5. Left (3,3) uncovered.\n\nColumn 4: points (4,1)-(4,4). Covered: (4,4) L2, (4,3) L3, (4,2) not covered, (4,1) L4. So (4,2) uncovered.\n\nColumn 5: points (5,1)-(5,3). Covered: (5,3) L2, (5,2) L3, (5,1) L5. All covered.\n\nColumn 6: points (6,1),(6,2). Covered: (6,2) L2, (6,1) L3. All covered.\n\nColumn 7: (7,1) covered by L2.\n\nRemaining uncovered points: (2,4),(3,3),(4,2). That's 3 points, to be covered by 2 sunny lines. Same situation as before. So we need to cover these three points with two sunny lines. Let's see if we can find a line that covers two of them and is sunny. Check pairs:\n\n- (2,4) and (3,3): slope (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (2,4) and (4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,3) and (4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nAll pairs have slope -1, which is non-sunny. So any line covering any two of these points is slope -1, not allowed. So we cannot cover any pair with a sunny line. So we need three sunny lines to cover them, but we only have two. So this arrangement fails.\n\nThus, for n=7, k=2 seems impossible with v=1. Let's try v=0 (no vertical lines). Then we have 5 non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1) and 2 sunny lines. So total non-vertical lines = 5 + 2 = 7. Each column can have at most 7 points covered, which is fine. We need to cover all points with 7 lines (5 non-sunny, 2 sunny). Since we have 7 lines total, each line can cover at most one point per