y x+y=5\n- (1,3) covered by x+y=4\n- (1,2) not covered (since x+y=3 would be (1,2) but we didn't include that line)\n- (1,1) not covered (x+y=2 would be (1,1) but not included)\n\nColumn 2:\n- (2,6) covered by x+y=8\n- (2,5) covered by x+y=7\n- (2,4) covered by x+y=6\n- (2,3) covered by x+y=5\n- (2,2) covered by x+y=4\n- (2,1) not covered (x+y=3 would be (2,1) but not included)\n\nColumn 3:\n- (3,5) covered by x+y=8\n- (3,4) covered by x+y=7\n- (3,3) covered by x+y=6\n- (3,2) covered by x+y=5\n- (3,1) covered by x+y=4\n\nColumn 4:\n- (4,4) covered by x+y=8\n- (4,3) covered by x+y=7\n- (4,2) covered by x+y=6\n- (4,1) covered by x+y=5\n\nColumn 5:\n- (5,3) covered by x+y=8\n- (5,2) covered by x+y=7\n- (5,1) covered by x+y=6\n\nColumn 6:\n- (6,2) covered by x+y=8\n- (6,1) covered by x+y=7\n\nColumn 7:\n- (7,1) covered by x+y=8\n\nThus uncovered points after the 5 slope -1 lines are:\n\n- (1,2),(1,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (maybe (2,?) no other)\n- That's it? Let's check column 2: (2,1) uncovered. Column 1: (1,2),(1,1) uncovered. All other points are covered. So we have exactly 3 uncovered points: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1). We have 2 sunny lines left to cover these 3 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we could assign one sunny line to cover (1,1) and (2,1) maybe? That's line through (1,1) and (2,1) horizontal, not allowed. So cannot. Could assign one sunny line to cover (1,1) and (1,2) vertical, not allowed. So we need to cover each point with a separate line, but we have only 2 lines. So we cannot cover all three points with only 2 sunny lines. So this construction fails.\n\nBut we could adjust the set of slope -1 lines to include x+y=3 (covering (1,2),(2,1)) and maybe drop one of the other lines. Let's try to use slope -1 lines x+y=8,7,6,5,3 (instead of 4). That would cover (1,2),(2,1) as well. Then we would have uncovered points: (1,1) only. Let's see.\n\nReplace x+y=4 line with x+y=3 line: x+y=3 covers (1,2),(2,1). Then uncovered points become (1,1) only (since (1,3) was covered by x+y=4 which we removed). Actually we removed x+y=4, so (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) become uncovered. Let's list coverage with lines x+y=8,7,6,5,3:\n\n- x+y=8: (1,7),(2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1)\n- x+y=7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)\n- x+y=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\n- x+y=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\n- x+y=3: (1,2),(2,1)\n\nNow uncovered points:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1) not covered, (1,3) not covered (since x+y=4 missing), (1,? ) (1,7-5 covered). So (1,1),(1,3) uncovered.\n\nColumn 2: (2,2) not covered (since x+y=4 missing), (2,? ) (2,6-1 covered). So (2,2) uncovered.\n\nColumn 3: (3,1) not covered (since x+y=4 missing). (3,5-2 covered). So (3,1) uncovered.\n\nColumn 4: all covered (4,4,3,2,1 covered by x+y=8,7,6,5 respectively). So column 4 fully covered.\n\nColumn 5: all covered (5,3,2,1 covered by x+y=8,7,6). So column 5 fully covered.\n\nColumn 6: (6,2) covered by x+y=8, (6,1) covered by x+y=7. So column 6 fully covered.\n\nColumn 7: (7,1) covered by x+y=8.\n\nThus uncovered points: (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1). That's 4 points. We have 2 sunny lines left. Can we cover these 4 points with 2 sunny lines? Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. The points are in columns: (1,1) col1, (1,3) col1, (2,2) col2, (3,1) col3. So we have two points in column 1, one in column 2, one in column 3. We need to assign them to 2 lines, each line can have at most one point in column 1. So we need to assign (1,1) to one line, (1,3) to the other line. Then we need to assign (2,2) and (3,1) to either of the lines, but each line can have at most one point per column, so we could assign (2,2) to the line that has (1,1) (different column), and (3,1) to the line that has (1,3) (different column). So we need to find lines that pass through (1,1) and (2,2) (line y = x, slope 1, sunny) and through (1,3) and (3,1) (line slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed). So the second line cannot be sunny if it passes through (1,3) and (3,1). Could we as